--- abstract

The JMAP base protocol (RFC8620) provides the ability to upload and download
arbitrary binary data.  This binary data is called a "blob", and can be used
in all other JMAP extensions.

This extension adds a method to expose blobs as a filesystem along with the
types of metadata that are provided by other remote filesystem protocols.

--- middle

# Introduction

JMAP ({{JMAP-CORE}} — JSON Meta Application Protocol) is a generic
protocol for synchronizing data between a client and a server.
It is optimized for mobile and web environments, and aims to
provide a consistent interface to different data types.

In the same way that JMAP Calendars ({{JMAP-CALENDARS}}) replaces
CalDAV ({{CALDAV}}) and JMAP Contacts ({{JMAP-CONTACTS}}) replaces
CardDAV ({{CARDDAV}}), this document replaces the use of WebDAV ({{WEBDAV}})
for remote filesystem access.

## Conventions Used in This Document

{::boilerplate bcp14-tagged}

# Addition to the Capabilities Object

The capabilities object is returned as part of the JMAP Session
object; see {{JMAP-CORE}}, Section 2.

This document defines an additional capability URI.

## urn:ietf:params:jmap:filenode

The capability `urn:ietf:params:jmap:filenode` being present in the
"accountCapabilities" property of an account represents support
for the FileNode datatype.  Servers that include the capability
in one or more "accountCapabilities" properties MUST also include
the property in the "capabilities" property.

The value of this property in the JMAP session "capabilities"
property MUST be an empty object.

The value of this property in an account's "accountCapabilities"
property is an object that MUST contain the following information
on server capabilities and permissions for that account:

* maxFileNodeDepth: "UnsignedInt\|null"

    The maximum depth of the FileNode hierarchy (that is, one more than
    the maximum number of ancestors a FileNode may have), or null for
    no limit.

* maxSizeFileNodeName: "UnsignedInt"

    The maximum length, in (UTF-8) octets, allowed for the name of a
    FileNode.  This MUST be at least 100, although it is recommended
    servers allow more.

* forbiddenNameChars: "String\|null"

    A string where each character is forbidden in FileNode names.
    If null, the server does not restrict name characters beyond
    the requirement that names be non-empty Net-Unicode strings.
    The server MUST reject names containing any of these
    characters with an "invalidProperties" error.

* forbiddenNodeNames: "String\[\]\|null"

    An array of complete names that the server will not accept
    as FileNode names, compared case-insensitively.  For
    example, `[".", "..", "CON", "PRN", "AUX", "NUL"]` would
    forbid common path-traversal names and Windows reserved
    device names.  If null, the server does not forbid any
    specific names.

* supportedNodeTypes: "String\[\]"

    The `@type` values that the server allows clients to create
    in this account (see "FileNode objects" below).  This MUST
    include "Directory" and "File".  It MAY include "SymLink" and
    any other value registered in the "JMAP FileNode Types"
    registry.  Clients MUST ignore values they do not recognise.

* fileNodeQuerySortOptions: "String\[\]"

    A list of all the values the server supports for the "property"
    field of the Comparator object in a "FileNode/query" sort (see
    "FileNode/query" below).  This MAY include properties the client does not
    recognise (for example, custom properties specified in a vendor
    extension).  Clients MUST ignore any unknown properties in the
    list.

* mayCreateTopLevelFileNode: "Boolean"

   If true, the user may create a FileNode (see "FileNode/set" below) in this
   account with a null parentId.  (Permission for creating a child of
   an existing FileNode is given by the "myRights" property on that
   FileNode.)

* webTrashUrl: "String\|null"

    The URL at which the folder with the "trash" role can be viewed
    on the web.  If null, there is no web URL available.

* caseInsensitiveNames: "Boolean"

    If true, the server treats file names as case-insensitive for
    all name collision checks, including the sibling uniqueness
    constraint and `onExists` handling.  The server preserves the
    original case as provided by the client.

* webUrlTemplate: "String\|null"

    A template by which any node can be viewed on the web, in URI
    Template (level 1) format {{URI-TEMPLATE}}.  The available
    variable is `{id}`, which is the FileNode id.  If null, there
    is no web URL available.

* webWriteUrlTemplate: "String\|null"

    A URI Template (level 1) {{URI-TEMPLATE}} for writing content
    to a FileNode with `@type` "File".  The available variable is `{id}`, which is
    the FileNode id.  If null, direct HTTP writes are not
    supported and clients must use FileNode/set to update content.
    See "Direct HTTP Write" below for the HTTP semantics.

### Capability Example

~~~json
{
  "urn:ietf:params:jmap:filenode": {
    "maxFileNodeDepth": 50,
    "maxSizeFileNodeName": 255,
    "fileNodeQuerySortOptions": [
      "name", "mediaType", "size", "created", "modified",
      "nodeType", "tree"
    ],
    "supportedNodeTypes": ["Directory", "File", "SymLink"],
    "forbiddenNameChars": "/<>:\"\\|?*",
    "forbiddenNodeNames": [".", "..", "CON", "PRN", "AUX",
      "NUL", "COM0", "COM1", "COM2", "COM3", "COM4", "COM5",
      "COM6", "COM7", "COM8", "COM9", "LPT0", "LPT1", "LPT2",
      "LPT3", "LPT4", "LPT5", "LPT6", "LPT7", "LPT8", "LPT9"
    ],
    "caseInsensitiveNames": false,
    "mayCreateTopLevelFileNode": false,
    "webTrashUrl": "https://files.example.com/trash",
    "webUrlTemplate": "https://files.example.com/view/{id}",
    "webWriteUrlTemplate": "https://files.example.com/write/{id}"
  }
}
~~~

# FileNode Data Type

A FileNode is a set of metadata which behaves similarly to an inode in
a filesystem.  In {{WEBDAV}} terminology a Directory corresponds to
a collection and a File to a resource.

The following JMAP Methods are selected by the
`urn:ietf:params:jmap:filenode` capability.

## FileNode objects

Every FileNode has a type, given by its `@type` property.  This
document defines three types: "File", "Directory", and "SymLink".
Each type has its own set of properties in addition to the
properties common to all FileNodes.

Properties that this document defines for one type MUST NOT
appear on a FileNode of another type; they are omitted rather
than set to null.  An attempt to create or update a FileNode
that sets a property not defined for its `@type` to a non-null
value is an "invalidProperties" error.  The server MUST ignore a
null value for such a property, both on create and on update.
This lets a client send the same set of properties for nodes of
any type.

As in {{JMAP-CORE}} Section 5.3, setting a property that has a
default value to null, on create or update, sets it to that
default, unless the property's description says otherwise.

FileNode/get has additional rules for these properties (see
"FileNode/get" below).  In particular, for a node the user can
discover but not read, it returns null for most properties, even
those whose type does not otherwise allow null (see "Access
Control").

### Common properties

The following properties are defined for every FileNode,
regardless of its type:

* @type: "String" (immutable)

    The type of the node.  Values are registered in the "JMAP
    FileNode Types" registry (see IANA Considerations).  This
    document defines the values "File", "Directory", and
    "SymLink".  This MUST be provided on creation.  Attempting to
    change `@type` after creation is an "invalidProperties"
    error.  A server MUST reject the creation of a node with an
    `@type` that is not in the `supportedNodeTypes` capability
    with an "invalidProperties" error.  Clients MUST be prepared to receive nodes with
    `@type` values they do not recognise, and SHOULD NOT
    interpret any of their properties other than the common
    properties.

* id: "Id" (immutable; server-set)

    The Id of the FileNode.

* parentId: "Id\|null"

    The Id of the parent node, or null if this is a top level
    node.  The parent MUST be a node whose `@type` permits
    children; of the types defined in this document, only
    "Directory" does.  Setting parentId to any other node is an
    "invalidProperties" error.

* name: "String"

    User-visible name for the FileNode.  This MUST be a
    Net-Unicode string {{UNICODE}} of at least 1 character in length,
    subject to the maximum size given in the capability object.  There
    MUST NOT be two sibling FileNodes with both the same parent and
    the same name.  The server MUST reject names containing any
    character listed in `forbiddenNameChars`, or matching any
    entry in `forbiddenNodeNames` (case-insensitively), with an
    "invalidProperties" error.  See Implementation Considerations
    for guidance on which characters and names to forbid.

* created: "UTCDate" (default: current server time)

    The date the node was created.

* modified: "UTCDate\|null" (default: current server time)

    The date the node was last updated.  The server does not
    automatically update this value.  If the client does not
    include `modified` in an update, the server MUST leave it
    unchanged.  If the client sets `modified` to null, the
    server MUST set it to the current server time.  See
    Implementation Considerations for usage guidance.

* accessed: "UTCDate\|null" (default: current server time)

    The date the node was last accessed.  As with `modified`,
    the server does not automatically update this value; clients
    SHOULD provide an updated value when appropriate.  If not
    included in an update, the server MUST leave it unchanged.
    If set to null, the server MUST set it to the current server
    time.


* changed: "UTCDate" (server-set)

    The date the server last recorded a change to any property
    of this node.  The server MUST automatically update this
    value whenever any property of the node is modified,
    including `blobId`, `name`, `parentId`, `shareWith`,
    `executable`, and all other mutable properties.  This is
    not settable by clients.

### Container properties

The following properties are defined for every FileNode whose
`@type` permits children; of the types defined in this document,
only "Directory" does.  They do not appear on other nodes, whose
rights come from their parent (see "Access Control").

* isSubscribed: "Boolean" (default: true)

    This property is stored per user, and if true, the node should
    be displayed to the current user.  Setting it changes only the
    current user's value, and requires no rights beyond being able
    to discover the node.

* myRights: "FilesRights" (server-set)

    The set of rights (ACLs) the user has in relation to this
    node.  A **FilesRights** object has the following properties:

    * mayRead: `Boolean` The user may read all properties and
      the blob content of this node.  Without it, some properties
      are still visible (see "Access Control").
    * mayAddChildren: `Boolean` The user may create child nodes
      in this directory.
    * mayRename: `Boolean` The user may rename or move this node.
      This is governed by the parent's rights (see "Access
      Control").
    * mayDelete: `Boolean` The user may destroy this node.  This
      is governed by the parent's rights (see "Access Control").
    * mayModifyContent: `Boolean` The user may update the
      content-related properties of this node (`blobId`, `mediaType`,
      `target`, `modified`, `accessed`, `executable`).
    * mayShare: `Boolean` The user may change the sharing of this
      node (see {{JMAP-SHARING}}).

* shareWith: "Id\[FilesRights]\|null"

    A map of userId to rights for users this node is shared with.
    This is the access control list set on this node only; rights
    inherited from ancestors do not appear in it (see "Access
    Control").  The owner of the node MUST NOT be in this set.
    This is `null` if the user requesting the object does not have
    `myRights.mayShare`, or if the node is not shared with anyone.

### File properties

A FileNode with `@type` "File" is a regular file with blob
content.  In addition to the common properties, it has the
following properties:

* blobId: "Id"

    The blobId for the content of this file.  This MUST be
    provided on creation, including for zero byte files.
    Setting blobId to null is an "invalidProperties" error.
    Updating the blobId replaces the file's content; the server
    MUST update `size` to match the new blob.  A blob referenced
    by a FileNode MUST NOT be expired or garbage collected by the
    server while the FileNode exists.

* size: "UnsignedInt" (server-set)

    The size in bytes of the associated blob data.  Size is
    optional on create and update, but if provided it MUST match
    the size of the provided blobId.  If the client updates
    `blobId` without providing `size`, the server sets `size`
    from the new blob and returns the size in the "updated"
    response.

* mediaType: "String" (default: "application/octet-stream")

    The media type of the file.  If not provided on creation,
    or if set to null, the server sets it to
    "application/octet-stream".  Valid values are found in the
    IANA media-types registry.  Servers MUST NOT reject media types that are not
    recognised.  Servers MUST reject media types if the value
    does not conform to the ABNF of {{MEDIATYPE}} Section 4.2.

* executable: "Boolean" (default: false)

    If true, the file should be treated as an executable by
    operating systems that support this flag.

### Directory properties

A FileNode with `@type` "Directory" is a collection that may
contain child nodes.  In addition to the common properties, it
has the following property:

* role: "String\|null" (default: null)

    An indication that this directory has a special role.  A
    directory with the "root" role should have a null parentId.
    Clients MUST ignore unrecognised role values.  Values are
    registered in the "JMAP FileNode Roles" registry (see IANA
    Considerations).

### SymLink properties

A FileNode with `@type` "SymLink" is a symbolic link to another
path in the tree.  In addition to the common properties, it has
the following property:

* target: "String\[\]"

    The target path of the symlink, as an array of path
    elements.  Each element is a node name in the path.  An
    empty string as the first element indicates an absolute
    path from the root of the tree; otherwise the path is
    relative to the symlink's parent.  An element of ".." refers
    to the parent directory.  The target is not required to
    resolve to an existing node (symlinks may dangle).  The
    target is mutable; updating it changes where the symlink
    points.  This MUST be provided on creation.  Setting
    target to null is an "invalidProperties" error.

### FileNode Examples

A directory, a file inside it, and a symlink pointing to the
file.  Common properties other than `@type`, `id`, `parentId`,
and `name` are omitted for brevity:

~~~json
[
  {
    "@type": "Directory",
    "id": "n1",
    "parentId": null,
    "name": "Documents",
    "role": "documents"
  },
  {
    "@type": "File",
    "id": "n3",
    "parentId": "n1",
    "name": "report.pdf",
    "blobId": "Bfa61c0",
    "size": 48576,
    "mediaType": "application/pdf",
    "executable": false
  },
  {
    "@type": "SymLink",
    "id": "n7",
    "parentId": null,
    "name": "latest-report",
    "target": ["Documents", "report.pdf"]
  }
]
~~~

## FileNode Methods

### FileNode/get

This is a standard Foo/get method, with the following differences:

* The `@type` property is always returned, even if it is not
  included in the `properties` argument.
* If the `properties` argument includes a property that is
  defined for some FileNode type but not for a node's `@type`,
  the server omits that property from that node in the response.
  It does not return the property with a null value.
* For a node the user can discover but not read, the server
  returns null for most properties (see "Access Control").

There is one additional request argument:

* fetchParents: "Boolean" (default: false)

    If true, the server returns all ancestor nodes of the
    requested ids in addition to the requested nodes themselves.
    This allows a client to reconstruct the full path to each
    requested node in a single call.  Ancestor nodes that have
    already been included (either because they were explicitly
    requested or because they are ancestors of multiple requested
    nodes) are not duplicated.  The ancestors are returned as
    additional entries in the `list` array.

### FileNode/changes

This is a standard Foo/changes method.

### FileNode/set

This is a standard Foo/set method, with the following differences:

Since parentId creates a tree structure, an attempt to move a node
to a parent for which this node is also an ancestor is an error,
and an `invalidProperties` error will be returned.

A server MUST order creation and deletion operations within a
single FileNode/set such that the uniqueness of sibling names is
retained at the end of the transaction, but
replacing an existing file can be done atomically.

There are these additional top level arguments:

* onDestroyRemoveChildren: "Boolean" (default: `false`)

   If false, an attempt to destroy a FileNode which is the parentId
   of another FileNode will be rejected with a `nodeHasChildren`
   error.  NOTE: if all the child nodes are being destroyed in the
   same operation, then the server MUST NOT return this error.
   Servers MUST either sort the destroys children before parents,
   or only check this constraint on the final state, remembering
   that JMAP `set` operations must be atomic.

   If true, then all child nodes will also be destroyed when a
   node is destroyed.  When deleting child nodes, the server MUST
   include the ids of all deleted nodes in the method response.

* onExists: "String\|null" (default: null)

   If null, an attempt to create or update a FileNode which would
   cause a name collision will be rejected by the server with an
   "alreadyExists" error.

   If "replace", the existing item will be destroyed.  In this
   case, the server MUST include the id of the replaced item in
   the destroyed response list.  NOTE: if the replaced item is a
   Directory which has children, then the server MUST respond with
   a nodeHasChildren error to this action unless
   onDestroyRemoveChildren is true.

   Replacement does not depend on the `@type` of either node; a
   node of any type may replace a node of any other type.

   If "rename", the server will change the "name" field to not
   clash, using an algorithm of its choice.  If the server changes
   the name, it MUST include the new "name" value in the created
   or updated response field for this id.

   If "newest", the server compares the `modified` timestamp of
   the incoming item with that of the existing FileNode.  If the
   incoming item has a strictly later `modified` value, the
   existing node is destroyed and the operation proceeds as with
   "replace".  Otherwise the operation is rejected as with null,
   returning an "alreadyExists" error with the `existingId` of the
   surviving node.

   If "unchanged", the operation proceeds as with "replace", but
   only if a precondition on the colliding node is satisfied.  The
   client supplies this precondition in the "ifUnchangedBy" argument
   ({{JMAP-CONDITIONAL}}).  The server matches it against the node
   that occupies the contested name at the start of the method,
   found by that node's current id: the server evaluates the
   "ifUnchangedBy" entry whose key is that id.  If "ifUnchangedBy"
   contains no entry for the occupant's id, or that entry's
   precondition is not satisfied, the server MUST NOT replace the
   node.  It rejects the operation as with null, and returns an
   "alreadyExists" error with the `existingId` of the surviving
   node.  This binds the replacement to the one node the client
   expected.  If a concurrent change has moved a different node into
   the contested name, its id does not match the supplied
   precondition.  The server then refuses the replacement, and does
   not destroy a node the client never saw.  As with "replace", if
   the node to replace is a Directory with children, the server MUST
   return a "nodeHasChildren" error unless onDestroyRemoveChildren
   is true; it MUST also include the id of the replaced node in the
   destroyed response list.  A server that does not support
   {{JMAP-CONDITIONAL}} MUST reject "unchanged" with an
   "invalidArguments" error.

   The server checks a non-null onExists value in this order, and
   stops at the first check that fails:

   1. If the existing node is not discoverable by the user (see
      "Access Control"), every value except "rename" fails with
      "forbidden": the server MUST NOT destroy or compare against
      that node, nor reveal its id.  Servers may return "forbidden"
      for other reasons, such as a file name policy, so it does not
      confirm that the name is taken.

   2. "replace", "newest", and "unchanged" require `mayDelete` on
      the existing node, and "newest" and "unchanged" also require
      `mayRead` on it.  Without these rights, the operation fails
      with "forbidden".

   3. Only after these checks does the server compare `modified`
      for "newest" or evaluate the precondition for "unchanged".

   4. If the existing node is to be replaced, the server then
      checks its children: the operation fails with
      "nodeHasChildren" if it has children and
      onDestroyRemoveChildren is false, or with "forbidden" if
      replacing it would remove nodes the user cannot discover
      (see "Access Control").

   This order means that a user who lacks the rights to replace a
   node gets "forbidden" whatever the values of that node's
   properties, so the result cannot be used to learn properties
   that FileNode/get would not return.

* compareCaseInsensitively: "Boolean" (default: false)

   If true, name collision checks (including `onExists` handling)
   treat names as case-insensitive for this request.  This has no
   effect if the server already has `caseInsensitiveNames` set to
   true in its capability.  This allows clients syncing to
   case-insensitive platforms to prevent name collisions that
   would be problematic on those platforms.

Errors (in addition to standard SetError codes from {{JMAP-CORE}}):

* "alreadyExists" — a create or update would cause a name collision
  with a discoverable sibling FileNode, and onExists did not
  resolve it.  The SetError object MUST include an `existingId`
  property with the id of the existing FileNode.
* "forbidden" — a name collision with a sibling the user cannot
  discover, an onExists replacement without the required rights on
  the existing node (see onExists above), or a destroy that would
  remove nodes the user cannot discover (see "Access Control").
* "invalidProperties" — an attempt was made to move a node to a
  descendant of itself, creating a cycle in the tree, to make a
  node the child of a node whose `@type` does not permit
  children, to change the `@type` of a node, or to set a
  property that is not defined for the node's `@type` to a
  non-null value.
* "nodeHasChildren" — a destroy was attempted on a FileNode that
  has children, and onDestroyRemoveChildren is false.

### FileNode/copy

This is a standard Foo/copy function with the same additional
top-level arguments as FileNode/set (onDestroyRemoveChildren,
onExists, and compareCaseInsensitively), with the same behaviour.

The response has one additional property:

* destroyed: "Id\[\]\|null"

    The ids of the FileNodes in the destination account that were
    destroyed because onExists replaced them, including any
    descendants removed with onDestroyRemoveChildren.  This is
    null if no FileNodes were destroyed.

Errors: the same additional errors as FileNode/set apply.

### FileNode/query

This is a standard Foo/query method except for the following:

There's one more property to the query:

* depth: "UnsignedInt\|null" (default: null)

    The maximum depth in the tree, counted from the top level, at
    which a node can match: nodes with a null parentId are at depth
    0, their children at depth 1, and so on.  This applies whatever
    the filter; conditions such as `parentId` and `ancestorId` only
    select among the nodes within it, and depth is never counted
    from them.  So `ancestorId` without `depth` matches the whole
    subtree.  If null, there is no limit.

The following filter criteria are defined:

* isTopLevel: "Boolean"

    If true, the node must have a null parentId to match the
    condition.

* parentId: "Id"

    A FileNode id.  A node must have a parentId equal to this to
    match the condition.

* ancestorId: "Id"

    A FileNode id.  A node must have an ancestor (parent, parent
    of parent, etc.) with an id equal to this to match the
    condition.

* descendantId: "Id"

    A FileNode id.  A node must be an ancestor (parent, parent
    of parent, etc.) of the node with this id to match the
    condition.  This is the inverse of `ancestorId`.

* nodeType: "String"

    A node type value.  Only nodes whose `@type` is precisely
    this value match this condition.  This filter, and the
    `nodeType` sort property below, are named `nodeType` rather
    than `@type`; they operate on the `@type` property.

* role: "String"

    A role name.  Only nodes with precisely this role match this
    condition.

* hasAnyRole: "Boolean"

    If true, any node with a defined role matches this condition.
    If false, any node which has a role does not match this
    condition.

* blobId: "Id"

    A FileNode must have a blobId equal to this to match the
    condition.

* isExecutable: "Boolean"

    If `true`, the FileNode must have a true executable value.

* createdBefore: "UTCDate"

    The creation date of the node must be before this date to
    match the condition.

* createdAfter: "UTCDate"

    The creation date of the node must be on or after this date
    to match the condition.

* modifiedBefore: "UTCDate"

    The modified date of the node must be before this date to
    match the condition.

* modifiedAfter: "UTCDate"

    The modified date of the node must be on or after this date
    to match the condition.

* accessedBefore: "UTCDate"

    The accessed date of the node must be before this date to
    match the condition.

* accessedAfter: "UTCDate"

    The accessed date of the node must be on or after this date
    to match the condition.

* minSize: "UnsignedInt"

    The size of the node in bytes must be equal to or greater
    than this number to match the condition.

* maxSize: "UnsignedInt"

    The size of the node in bytes must be less than this number
    to match the condition.

* name: "String"

    A FileNode must have exactly the same octets in its name
    property to match the condition.

* nameMatch: "String"

    Matches the *name* property of the node using glob syntax.
    The following special patterns are defined: `*` matches any
    sequence of characters, `?` matches any single character,
    and a pair of brackets matches any single character in the
    bracketed set (for example, `[abc]` matches "a", "b", or "c";
    `[a-z]` matches any lowercase letter; `[!abc]` or `[^abc]`
    matches any character not in the set).  All other characters match
    literally.  The match is case-insensitive.

* mediaType: "String"

    A FileNode must have exactly the same octets in its mediaType
    property to match the condition.

* mediaTypeMatch: "String"

    Matches the *mediaType* property of the node using the same
    glob syntax as *nameMatch*.

* body: "String"

    Match the content of the referenced blob, see the definition
    of *body* in section 4.4.1 of {{JMAP-MAIL}}.  The server may
    use any technology to extract meaningful text from the blob
    for searching, or interpret the string in any way, to choose
    the nodes that it believes the user wants to see.

* text: "String"

    Is equivalent to *body* OR *nameMatch* OR *mediaTypeMatch*.

The blobId, isExecutable, minSize, maxSize, mediaType,
mediaTypeMatch, and
body conditions match only nodes whose `@type` is "File".  The
role and hasAnyRole conditions match only nodes whose `@type` is
"Directory".  This applies to both true and false values.  For
example, a hasAnyRole condition of false matches only Directories
without a role, and an isExecutable condition of false matches
only Files that are not executable.

For a node the user can discover but not read, a condition on a
property that FileNode/get returns as null for that node (see
"Access Control") does not match the node, whatever its value.
The body condition, and the body part of the text condition,
never match such a node.

It also supports the following additional sort properties:

* tree:

    Sort by tree; which means by name, but any Directory node is
    immediately followed by the recursive application of
    the same sort to its child nodes.  This is similar to the
    output of the `find` command on a filesystem with the depth
    parameter provided above.

* nodeType:

    Sort by `@type`.  Directories sort first, then symlinks,
    then files, then nodes of any other `@type` ordered by the
    `@type` value.

When sorting by a property that is not defined for a node's
`@type` (for example `size` on a Directory), or that FileNode/get
returns as null because the user cannot read the node (see
"Access Control"), nodes without the property sort before
nodes that have it when isAscending is true.  Nodes that lack the property are ordered among themselves
as if that Comparator were absent from the sort.

### FileNode/queryChanges

This is a standard Foo/queryChanges method, which also takes the
`depth` argument of FileNode/query, with the same meaning.

# Direct HTTP Write

When the server advertises a `webWriteUrlTemplate`, clients can
update the content of a File directly via HTTP without using
FileNode/set.  The URL is constructed by expanding the template
with the FileNode id.  This is only valid for nodes with
`@type` "File"; requests targeting any other type of node MUST
be rejected with an HTTP 400 status.

The client MUST have `mayModifyContent` permission on the node.
Requests without sufficient permission MUST be rejected with an
HTTP 403 status.  Authentication uses the same mechanism as other
JMAP HTTP endpoints.

## PUT

A PUT request replaces the entire content of the node.  The
request body is the new content.  The Content-Type header sets
the `mediaType` of the node.  If the request has no Content-Type
header, the server sets `mediaType` to "application/octet-stream".

On success, the server MUST update the node's `blobId`, `size`,
and `mediaType` properties, and return an HTTP 200 response with a
JSON body:

~~~json
{
  "blobId": "Bnewblob123",
  "size": 48576,
  "mediaType": "text/plain"
}
~~~

## PATCH

A PATCH request applies a partial modification to the node's
current content.  The Content-Type of the request indicates the
patch format.  The supported patch formats are those listed in
the `supportedPatchTypes` capability of {{JMAP-BLOBEXT}} (for example,
`application/x-rdiff-delta`, `application/x-bsdiff`,
`text/x-diff`).  The server applies the delta to the node's
current blob to produce the new content.

On success, the server MUST update the node's `blobId` and
`size` properties.  The server MUST NOT change the node's
`mediaType`.  A client that needs to change it uses PUT, or
updates `mediaType` with FileNode/set.  The response is an HTTP
200 with the same JSON body as PUT:

~~~json
{
  "blobId": "Bpatched456",
  "size": 49152,
  "mediaType": "text/plain"
}
~~~

Servers that do not support a given patch format MUST respond
with HTTP 415 (Unsupported Media Type).

# Access Control

Access control lists are set with `shareWith`, which only nodes
whose `@type` permits children can have.  Rights are inherited
down the tree: a node's `myRights` is the union of the rights
granted to the user by the `shareWith` entries on the node and on
all its ancestors.  Inherited rights are a floor.  A `shareWith`
entry on a descendant can grant a user more rights on that
subtree, but it cannot take away rights granted higher up.

A node whose `@type` does not permit children has no `myRights`.
The user's rights on it are those in its parent's `myRights`;
`mayAddChildren` and `mayShare` do not apply to it.  Such a node
with a null parentId is discoverable only by the account owner,
who has all rights on it.

Renaming, moving or destroying a node changes the contents of its
parent, so it is governed by the parent's rights: the server checks
`mayRename` (to rename or move the node) and `mayDelete` (to
destroy it) in the parent's `myRights`.  Accordingly, a Directory's
own `myRights.mayRename` and `myRights.mayDelete` report its
parent's values.  A Directory with a role cannot be destroyed,
renamed or moved, and reports `mayRename` and `mayDelete` as false.

When a `shareWith` change on a node causes the derived `myRights`
to change for any descendant, the server MUST report those
descendants as changed in FileNode/changes responses.  The server
SHOULD NOT report descendants whose derived `myRights` did not
actually change, but MAY do so if it cannot efficiently determine
whether the derived values differ.  In particular, a server MAY
treat a `shareWith` change as a change to every node in that
subtree, including nodes that have no `myRights` of their own.

Nodes which are not "discoverable" MUST return notFound errors if
fetched with FileNode/get and MUST NOT be returned in response to
FileNode/query.  Nodes are discoverable in one of two ways:

1. If the node or an ancestor of the node has mayRead true.

2. If the node or a descendant of the node has a `shareWith`
   entry that grants the user any right.

When a node stops being discoverable by the user, for example
because a `shareWith` entry was removed, the server reports it as
destroyed in FileNode/changes.

A node that is discoverable only through the second rule has
mayRead false.  It is visible so that the user can use the
rights granted on it, and can see the full path to the nodes
they have access to.  This means that
a query with that node as the parent will only return those of its
children which are otherwise discoverable through the second
discoverability rule.

For a node that is discoverable but does not have `mayRead`,
FileNode/get returns the real values of `id`, `@type`, `name`,
`parentId`, `myRights`, `isSubscribed`, and `shareWith` (which
follows its own rule for `mayShare`).  It returns null for every
other property defined for the node's `@type`, including
`created`, `changed`, and `role`, even where that property's type
does not otherwise allow null.  A change to a property the user
sees as null is not visible to the user, so the server SHOULD NOT
report the node as updated in FileNode/changes because of it, but
MAY do so if it cannot efficiently determine whether any property
visible to the user changed.

Such a node can have descendants the user cannot discover.  A
destroy that would remove any of them, either through
onDestroyRemoveChildren or as part of a node replaced through
onExists, MUST be rejected with "forbidden", and the server MUST
NOT destroy any node in that subtree.  Without
onDestroyRemoveChildren, destroying such a node while it has
children fails with "nodeHasChildren" whether or not the user can
discover those children.

This leads to a sharee seeing the full path to anything that they have
access to, but nothing else.

For example, in a unix homedirectory environment where user Alice had shared
the "forBob" folder, one might see, when logged in as Bob:

~~~artwork
/home
  /alice
    /shared
      /forBob
        file1.jpg
        file2.txt
        ...
  /bob
    bobfile.txt
    ...
~~~

While Alice would see many more files and folders at the home
directory level.

This does lead to potentially large changes in the visible Node
set, so when Alice first shared that directory, Bob would have
been told of a large number of new Nodes in response to a
FileNode/changes query.  The server might report these as
"created" or "updated" — it is not always possible for the server
to know whether a node was previously visible to the user.

# Quotas

Servers that support JMAP Quotas ({{JMAP-QUOTAS}}) SHOULD
provide Quota objects with `"FileNode"` in the `types` array.
Both the `octets` resource type (total storage used by file
node blobs) and the `count` resource type (number of FileNode
objects) are applicable.

# Integration with JMAP Blob Extensions

When a server advertises both `urn:ietf:params:jmap:filenode` and
`urn:ietf:params:jmap:blob2`, the ArchiveEntry object type
(defined in {{JMAP-BLOBEXT}}) is extended with two additional
properties:

* nodeId: "Id\|null"
  A FileNode id.  When present, the server populates the
  archive entry from the referenced FileNode's properties.
  The `blobId` and `name` are taken from the FileNode.  The
  FileNode's `created` and `modified` timestamps map to the
  archive entry's time metadata.  The user's rights on the
  FileNode (specifically the `mayModifyContent` right) and,
  for a File, the `executable` property inform the `mode` field in the
  archive entry (for example, permissions and executable bit).
  If the ArchiveEntry also provides explicit values for
  any of these properties, the explicit values take
  precedence.  Mutually exclusive with providing `blobId`
  directly when the FileNode's `@type` is "File".

* recurse: "Boolean" (default: false)
  Only valid when the referenced FileNode's `@type` is
  "Directory".
  When true, the server recursively includes all children
  of the directory in the archive.  Each child entry's
  `name` is its full path relative to the directory.
  Directory names MUST include a trailing `/`.

A FileNode with `@type` "SymLink", whether referenced directly by
`nodeId` or reached by `recurse`, is added to the archive as an
entry with `entryType` "symlink".  The server never follows the
symlink.  The entry's `linkTarget` is the elements of the
SymLink's `target` joined with "/".  An absolute target, whose
first element is the empty string, therefore produces a
`linkTarget` that starts with "/".

Some archive formats cannot hold every node type.  For example,
"application/zip" has no symlink entries.  The ArchiveRecipe
(defined in {{JMAP-BLOBEXT}}) is extended with one additional
property that controls how the server handles such nodes:

* onUnsupportedEntry: "String" (default: "error")
  This applies to any node, referenced by `nodeId` or reached by
  `recurse`, that the archive format cannot represent.  If
  "error", the server rejects the Blob/convert creation with an
  "unsupportedEntry" SetError, and creates no archive.  If
  "skip", the server leaves such nodes out of the archive.  Any
  other value is an "invalidProperties" error.

This property applies only to entries that the server builds
from FileNodes.  An entry that the client gives in full, with an
unsupported `entryType`, is still an "invalidProperties" error as
{{JMAP-BLOBEXT}} requires.

If `nodeId` references a FileNode that the user does not have
permission to read, or that does not exist, the server MUST
reject the Blob/convert creation with a `notFound` SetError.

## Examples

To archive an entire directory tree rooted at FileNode
"dirabc" into a zip file:

```json
[["Blob/convert", {
  "accountId": "account1",
  "create": {
    "myarchive": {
      "archive": {
        "type": "application/zip",
        "entries": [{
          "nodeId": "dirabc",
          "name": "/",
          "recurse": true
        }]
      }
    }
  }
}, "R1"]]
```

To archive specific files from different locations along with
a full subdirectory:

```json
[["Blob/convert", {
  "accountId": "account1",
  "create": {
    "selective": {
      "archive": {
        "type": "application/zip",
        "entries": [
          {
            "nodeId": "filenode1",
            "name": "README.txt"
          },
          {
            "nodeId": "dirnode2",
            "name": "docs/",
            "recurse": true
          }
        ]
      }
    }
  }
}, "R1"]]
```

## Extracting Archives into FileNodes

When both capabilities are present, the ExtractRecipe
(defined in {{JMAP-BLOBEXT}}) is extended with the following
additional properties:

* parentNodeId: "Id\|null"
  The id of a FileNode with `@type` "Directory".  When
  present, the server extracts the archive contents into this
  directory, creating FileNode objects for each entry.
  Subdirectories in the archive are created as FileNodes with
  `@type` "Directory".  File entries become FileNodes with
  `@type` "File" and their `blobId` set to the extracted
  content.  The `created` and `modified` timestamps are taken
  from the archive entry metadata.  If the archive entry
  includes a `mode` field, the server SHOULD use it to set the
  `executable` property on the created FileNode.

  Symlink entries become FileNodes with `@type` "SymLink" if
  "SymLink" is in the `supportedNodeTypes` capability.  The
  server splits the entry's `linkTarget` on "/" to produce the
  `target` path elements.  A `linkTarget` that starts with "/"
  produces an empty string as the first element, which marks an
  absolute path.  The server drops any other empty elements.

  An entry is unsupported if the server cannot create a FileNode
  for it.  These are symlink entries when "SymLink" is not in
  `supportedNodeTypes`, and entries of any type that has no
  FileNode equivalent ("hardlink", "fifo", "blockDevice", and
  "charDevice").  The `onUnsupportedEntry` property controls how
  the server handles them.

* onUnsupportedEntry: "String" (default: "error")
  If "error", the server rejects the Blob/convert creation with
  an "unsupportedEntry" SetError, and creates no FileNodes.  If
  "skip", the server does not create a FileNode for an
  unsupported entry, and extracts the other entries as usual.  A
  skipped entry still appears in the response `entries` array,
  with a null `nodeId`.  Any other value is an
  "invalidProperties" error.

* onExists: "String\|null" (default: null)
  Controls the behaviour when an extracted entry would collide
  with an existing FileNode name under the same parent.  The
  values and semantics are the same as for FileNode/set (see
  above): null rejects with an `alreadyExists` SetError,
  "replace" destroys the existing node, "rename" gives the new
  node a server-chosen name, and "newest" replaces the existing
  node only if the archive entry has a strictly later `modified`
  timestamp.

When `parentNodeId` is set, each ArchiveEntry in the response
`entries` array includes an additional `nodeId` property
containing the id of the FileNode that was created for that
entry, or null if the server skipped the entry.

If `parentNodeId` references a FileNode that does not exist,
is not a Directory, or for which the user does not have
`mayAddChildren` permission, the server MUST reject the Blob/convert
creation with an appropriate SetError (`notFound`,
`invalidProperties`, or `forbidden`).

### Example

Extracting a zip file into a directory:

```json
[["Blob/convert", {
  "accountId": "account1",
  "create": {
    "e1": {
      "extract": {
        "blobId": "Barchive123",
        "type": "application/zip",
        "parentNodeId": "dir456"
      }
    }
  }
}, "R1"]]
```

The response includes a `nodeId` for each created entry:

```json
[["Blob/convert", {
  "accountId": "account1",
  "created": {
    "e1": {
      "id": "Barchive123",
      "type": "application/zip",
      "size": 104857,
      "entries": [
        {
          "name": "docs/",
          "entryType": "directory",
          "nodeId": "node001"
        },
        {
          "name": "docs/readme.txt",
          "blobId": "Bdd001",
          "entryType": "file",
          "modified": "2026-03-01T12:00:00Z",
          "mode": "0644",
          "nodeId": "node002"
        },
        {
          "name": "docs/logo.png",
          "blobId": "Bdd002",
          "entryType": "file",
          "modified": "2026-02-15T09:30:00Z",
          "mode": "0644",
          "nodeId": "node003"
        }
      ]
    }
  },
  "notCreated": {}
}, "R1"]]
```

## The unsupportedEntry Error

An "unsupportedEntry" SetError means that an archive or
extraction with `onUnsupportedEntry` set to "error" found one or
more entries that it could not handle.  The SetError object MUST
include an `entryNames` property: a "String\[\]" that lists the
`name` of each such entry.  For an archive, this is the name that
the entry would have had in the archive.

# Extended Attributes and Named Streams

Operating systems associate named side-channel data with files: POSIX extended
attributes (getxattr/setxattr), macOS extended attributes (including Finder
tags and the quarantine flag) and resource forks, NTFS and SMB2 alternate data
streams, and NFSv4 named attributes.  A FileNode carries these using the JMAP
Object Metadata extension ({{JMAP-METADATA}}).  A server that supports named
attributes on FileNodes lists "FileNode" in the "dataTypes" of its
`urn:ietf:params:jmap:metadata` capability.  Clients then read and write the
attributes through the "metadata" property, and through the "privateMetadata"
property where the server sets `supportsPrivate`.  That extension adds both
properties to the FileNode object.  They are common properties, available on
FileNodes of every `@type`, because platforms also attach named attributes to
directories and symbolic links.

## The "xattr" namespace

This document defines the "xattr" namespace for the "metadata" and
"privateMetadata" properties.  Its value is an object mapping an attribute name
to an **XattrValue** object.  The attribute name is the platform-native name of
the attribute or stream, stored verbatim (for example "user.comment",
"com.apple.metadata:_kMDItemUserTags", or "Zone.Identifier").  Servers and
clients MUST NOT normalise attribute names across platforms.  The server keeps
an attribute that one platform writes, even where another platform has no
equivalent for it.

An **XattrValue** object has the following properties:

* data: "String\|null"

    The attribute value as a UTF-8 string.  Used for small textual attributes.

* blobId: "Id\|null"

    The blobId of a blob holding the attribute value.  Used for large or
    binary attributes (for example a resource fork, or a multi-kilobyte
    stream).  A blob referenced by an XattrValue MUST NOT be expired or garbage
    collected by the server while the FileNode references it, in the same way
    as the content blob of a File.

* type: "String\|null" (default: null)

    An optional media type ({{MEDIATYPE}}) for the attribute value.

An XattrValue MUST contain exactly one of "data" or "blobId"; providing both,
or neither, is an "invalidProperties" error.  Binary attributes small enough to
carry inline are still referenced by "blobId" rather than base64-encoded in
"data".

The value nests one object level below the namespace value (attribute name to
XattrValue).  A server that supports the "xattr" namespace MUST advertise a
"maxDepth" for FileNode of at least 1, or null (no limit).

Attributes shared with every reader of the node (for example a quarantine flag)
are stored under "metadata".  Attributes private to the current user (for
example that user's own tags) are stored under "privateMetadata" where the
server supports it.

Example — a small textual attribute and a large binary one:

~~~json
{
  "metadata": {
    "xattr": {
      "user.comment": { "data": "draft copy" },
      "com.apple.ResourceFork": {
        "blobId": "G_fork_9c2",
        "type": "application/octet-stream"
      }
    }
  }
}
~~~

To set or remove a single attribute without disturbing the others, a client
patches the path directly.  RFC 6901 escaping applies to any "/" or "~" in the
attribute name ("/" as "~1", "~" as "~0"):

~~~json
[["FileNode/set", {
  "accountId": "u1",
  "update": {
    "f42": {
      "metadata/xattr/Zone.Identifier": { "data": "ZoneId=3" }
    }
  }
}, "0"]]
~~~

Setting an attribute's path to null removes it, following the "metadata" patch
semantics of {{JMAP-METADATA}}.

# Implementation Considerations

## Modification Timestamps

Clients SHOULD provide an updated `modified` value when changing
the content of a node.  This gives clients control over the
timestamp, for example when preserving original modification times
during file synchronization.  Clients that do not care about
preserving timestamps can set `modified` to null to have the server
use the current time.

Renaming or moving a node does not change its `modified` time, as
on common local filesystems.  The server MUST NOT change `modified`
for an update that does not include it, and a client SHOULD NOT
include `modified` in an update that only renames or moves a node
unless it intends to change it (for example by setting it to null).

Clients may also choose to update the `modified` timestamp on a
parent directory when moving a child node in or out, to reflect
that the directory's contents have changed.

## Timestamp Precision

The UTCDate type (RFC 3339) supports fractional seconds.
Servers SHOULD include sub-second precision in `created`,
`modified`, `accessed`, and `changed` timestamps where their
storage backend supports it.  Clients MUST preserve fractional
seconds when they send timestamps back to the server.

A server MAY store timestamps with less precision than a client
sends, down to whole seconds.  It then truncates the value it
stores, and returns the stored value in FileNode/get, so a client
can learn the precision in use.  Clients comparing a local time
with a server timestamp (for example to recognise their own
changes) need to allow for this.  Comparisons the server makes,
such as for the "newest" value of `onExists`, use the stored
precision.

## Filename Character Restrictions

Servers should set `forbiddenNameChars` and `forbiddenNodeNames`
to include characters and names that are problematic for their
storage backend and for the broadest set of clients.

The following characters are problematic on common platforms:

* Windows: `<`, `>`, `:`, `"`, `\`, `|`, `?`, `*`, and
  control characters (U+0000-U+001F).  Windows also forbids
  trailing dots and spaces in names.
* macOS: `:` (HFS+ legacy; displayed as `/` in Finder).
* Linux: the null byte (U+0000).

The following names are problematic:

* All platforms: `.` and `..` (path traversal).
* Windows: reserved device names CON, PRN, AUX, NUL,
  COM0-COM9, and LPT0-LPT9.  These are reserved regardless
  of file extension (for example, "CON.txt" is also forbidden).

A server targeting broad client compatibility should include
at least `/<>:"\|?*` in `forbiddenNameChars` and at least
`.`, `..`, and the Windows reserved names in
`forbiddenNodeNames`.

## Case Sensitivity

Windows and macOS filesystems are typically case-insensitive
(treating "Foo" and "foo" as the same name), while Linux
filesystems are typically case-sensitive.  Clients syncing to
case-insensitive platforms should use `compareCaseInsensitively`
on FileNode/set requests to prevent creating sibling nodes
whose names differ only in case, as these cannot coexist on
the target platform.

## Access Time Updates

Updating `accessed` on every file read generates significant
API traffic for little value.  Clients are encouraged to use a
"relatime" strategy similar to modern POSIX filesystems: only
update `accessed` if the current value is older than `modified`,
or if more than 24 hours have elapsed since the last `accessed`
update.  Clients should batch access time updates with their
next JMAP request rather than making a dedicated call.

# Security considerations

All security considerations from {{JMAP-CORE}} apply to this document.

## Path Traversal

Clients that reconstruct filesystem paths from FileNode hierarchies
MUST validate the resulting paths and reject any that would escape
the intended directory tree.  Servers SHOULD include `/` in
`forbiddenNameChars` and `.` and `..` in `forbiddenNodeNames` to
prevent path traversal attacks via node names.

## Symlink Targets

Symlink targets are not validated by the server to point to
existing nodes.  Clients that resolve symlinks MUST guard
against symlink loops (a symlink pointing to an ancestor of
itself) and MUST NOT follow symlinks outside the FileNode tree.
Servers SHOULD impose a limit on the length of `target` values
and MAY reject targets containing ".." path components.

## Denial of Service

Deep nesting of FileNodes or very large numbers of children under a
single parent can consume significant server resources.  Servers
SHOULD enforce reasonable limits via `maxFileNodeDepth` and MAY
impose additional limits on the number of children per node.
Recursive operations such as destroying a subtree with
`onDestroyRemoveChildren` can be expensive; servers SHOULD impose
limits on the size of subtrees that can be destroyed in a single
operation.

## Access Control

The discoverability rules defined in this document mean that ancestor
nodes of shared content are visible (with `mayRead` false) to users
who have access to descendants.  While these ancestor nodes do not
expose file content, their names and structure may reveal information
about the file hierarchy.  Server administrators and users sharing
content should be aware of this.

A user who can destroy a node, but cannot discover all of its
descendants, can still learn that the node has children: the
destroy fails with "nodeHasChildren", or with "forbidden" when
onDestroyRemoveChildren is true.  Any failure reveals this.
The alternatives would be to reveal the ids of the hidden nodes,
or to destroy data the user cannot see, and both are worse.

Changes to the `shareWith` property on a node affect all descendants.
Removing sharing from a parent node will make its descendants
undiscoverable to the affected users, unless a descendant is
also shared with them directly, which may be surprising if
those users had been actively working with the shared files.

## Content Security

Servers that perform content scanning or malware detection SHOULD
scan blobs referenced by FileNode objects.  The `mediaType` property is
client-asserted and MUST NOT be trusted for security decisions;
servers SHOULD independently verify content types where this matters.

