# Changes

EDITOR: please remove this section before publication.

The source of this document exists on github at: https://github.com/brong/draft-gondwana-jmap-filenode/

**draft-ietf-jmap-filenode-19**

* The query `depth` argument is counted from the top level of the
  account (top-level nodes are at depth 0), whatever the filter, and
  absent or null now means no limit rather than "do not recurse".
  FileNode/queryChanges takes it too.
* Renaming, moving or destroying a node is governed by its parent's
  `mayRename` and `mayDelete`, which a Directory's own `myRights`
  report.  A Directory with a role cannot be destroyed, renamed or
  moved.
* A node that stops being discoverable is reported as destroyed in
  FileNode/changes.
* `isSubscribed` is per user, and setting it needs no rights beyond
  discovering the node.
* A server may treat a `shareWith` change as a change to every node
  in the subtree, including nodes without `myRights`.
* Fixed the blob capability name in the ArchiveEntry text to
  `urn:ietf:params:jmap:blob2`, matching {{JMAP-BLOBEXT}}.
* Renaming or moving a node does not change its `modified` time;
  the guidance to update `modified` now applies to content changes.
* A server may store timestamps with less precision than clients
  send, down to whole seconds, truncating and returning the stored
  value; "newest" compares at that precision.

**draft-ietf-jmap-filenode-18**

* `isSubscribed`, `myRights`, and `shareWith` are defined only for
  nodes whose `@type` permits children.  Other nodes take their
  rights from their parent, so a sharing change no longer marks
  every descendant File as changed.
* `shareWith` is the access control list on that node only;
  `myRights` is the union of the lists on the node and its
  ancestors, so a descendant cannot narrow inherited rights.
* A node with a `shareWith` entry for the user, or with such a
  descendant, is discoverable.
* Defined which properties FileNode/get returns for a node that
  is discoverable but not readable, and how FileNode/query
  filters and sorts treat it; body never matches it.
* A destroy that would remove undiscoverable nodes fails with
  "forbidden".
* onExists checks rights before comparing against the existing
  node; "newest" and "unchanged" also require `mayRead`.
* FileNode/copy returns a `destroyed` list for nodes replaced
  through onExists.

**draft-ietf-jmap-filenode-17**

* Setting a property with a default to null resets it to that
  default (as in RFC 8620), for example `created` to the current
  server time and `isSubscribed` to true.
* Setting `mediaType` to null now resets it to
  "application/octet-stream" rather than being an error.
  (Thanks to Mark Atwood.)

**draft-ietf-jmap-filenode-16**

* A name collision with an undiscoverable node returns "forbidden"
  without `existingId`; only onExists "rename" proceeds.  (Thanks to
  Mehdi Kouhen.)
* onExists replacement requires `mayDelete` on the existing node.

**draft-ietf-jmap-filenode-15**

* Replaced `nodeType` with an `@type` property
  (File, Directory, SymLink).  Each type defines only the
  properties relevant to it; properties of other types are
  omitted rather than null.  `@type` is now required on
  creation instead of being inferred.
* Moved `blobId`, `size`, `type`, and `executable` to File,
  `role` to Directory, and `target` to SymLink.
* Renamed the File `type` property to `mediaType`, with a default
  of "application/octet-stream", so that it is not confused with
  `@type`.  Renamed the `type` and `typeMatch` filters to
  `mediaType` and `mediaTypeMatch`, and the `type` sort property
  to `mediaType`.
* Added the `supportedNodeTypes` capability.
* Removed the `X-FileNode-Type` header field.  A Direct HTTP Write
  PATCH no longer changes `mediaType`; clients use PUT or FileNode/set.
* Defined how SymLinks are archived (as symlink entries, never
  followed) and extracted (created as SymLinks if supported).
* Added `onUnsupportedEntry` ("error", the default, or "skip") to the
  archive and extract recipes, for entries the archive format or the
  server cannot represent, and the "unsupportedEntry" SetError.
* Defined the order among nodes that lack a sort property.
* Breaking change: the node type values changed from "file",
  "directory", and "symlink" to "File", "Directory", and "SymLink".
  Clients MUST now provide `@type` on creation.
* FileNode/get always returns `@type`, and omits requested
  properties that are not defined for a node's `@type`.
* Query filters on type-specific properties now match only nodes
  of that type, for both true and false values.  For example,
  `hasAnyRole: false` no longer matches Files or SymLinks, and
  `isExecutable: false` no longer matches Directories or SymLinks.
* Added "unchanged" value for `onExists`, replacing a colliding node only
  when a precondition on that node, supplied via "ifUnchangedBy"
  ({{JMAP-CONDITIONAL}}), is satisfied.  The precondition binds to the current
  id of the node that occupies the contested name, so a client can only
  replace the one node it expected.
* Added Extended Attributes and Named Streams: file nodes carry POSIX xattrs,
  macOS extended attributes, NTFS/SMB alternate data streams, and NFSv4 named
  attributes via the JMAP Object Metadata extension ({{JMAP-METADATA}}),
  defining the "xattr" namespace (attribute name to an XattrValue holding
  either inline "data" or a "blobId" for large/binary values).  Registered
  the "xattr" namespace with IANA.
* Escaped bracket characters in "String\[\]" type signatures.
* Editorial: applied a subset of ASD-STE100 Simplified Technical English —
  removed "e.g."/"i.e.", split over-long sentences (notably `onExists`
  "unchanged"), and reworded noun clusters and idioms.  No normative change.

